  <page_number>36</page_number>
  <running_head>I. IACOMOTI</running_head>
  <main_text>
    <p cont="true"><i>Hostisque dum fraudes recludit,</i></p>
    <p><i>Quae mala quósve tulit labores!</i></p>
    <p><i>Nulla rigenti frigore territum</i></p>
    <p><i>Videre Bruma aut Andromedes Pater,</i></p>
    <p><i>Et stella vesani Leonis,</i></p>
    <p><i>Cum segetes coquit æstus almas.</i></p>
    <p><i>Tantisque tandem mortibus addue,</i></p>
    <p><i>Condæe Princeps, quo tua carmine</i></p>
    <p><i>Dicenda, quo fletu riganda</i></p>
    <p><i>Fata truci properata fraude?</i></p>
    <p><i>Non præliantem perdidit hosticus</i></p>
    <p><i>Ensis duello, classica cum sonant,</i></p>
    <p><i>Armata quando militantes</i></p>
    <p><i>Per medias rapit ira cædes.</i></p>
    <p><i>Sed tu occidisti progenies bona</i></p>
    <p><i>Magni parentis, Borbonicæ altera</i></p>
    <p><i>Spes gentis occultis veneni</i></p>
    <p><i>Insidiis superatus atri,</i></p>
    <p><i>Quo delibutis muneribus ferox</i></p>
    <p><i>Medea quondam Colchica pellicem</i></p>
    <p><i>Est ulta, quo parens superbis</i></p>
    <p><i>Sævit imperiis Locusta.</i></p>
    <p><i>Has prisca fraudes Gallia nesciens</i></p>
    <p><i>Virtute sola &amp; Marte potentior</i></p>
    <p><i>Late per obstantes caternas</i></p>
    <p><i>Explicuit sua signa victrix.</i></p>
    <p><i>Hinc sordidatis vestibus horridæ</i></p>
    <p><i>Lugent Camœnæ, flent Charitum chori,</i></p>
    <p><i>Et</i></p>
  </main_text>
  <notes>The fragment "Et" at the lower right is a line-start beginning the next stanza, set outside the text block; treated as a separate fragment. Long s rendered as s; italic throughout.</notes>
</page>
